Z||000000||theogre||02-05-2001||05:39 PM||||If you read Cnet or other Tech news servers then you've likely seen the building issues over Juno's new policy forcing you to take a new peice of software in order to keep using the system for free.<P>This software, called a distributed computing client, allows Juno to use your machine to process whatever data they see fit. It has raised a number of issues over privacy.<P>Juno’s distributed computing plan has problems in addition to privacy. Many articles about Juno and its new user policy to force subscriber machines to perform distributed computing tasks focus on security and privacy issues but there is a direct environmental consequence to this as well.<P>Practically all computer equipment sold in the U.S. conforms to the USEPA Energy Star Program. As many people know, the Energy Star program is intended to reduce power consumption by various personal-computing devices.  In fact, it’s very hard to find a retail computer that doesn’t conform to this program.<P>In order for the Juno distributed computing software to operate, it must interfere with various aspects of a computer’s power management system.  Instead of the computer shutting off it’s hard drive and other modules to save power, the Juno software will have to keep some or all these modules running at full power in order to operate.<P>At first glance, this might seem trivial however this is a huge draw of power spread over a couple million computers. For the sake of example, if a PC uses only 1 extra watt because of this software then Juno is consuming over Two Million watts of power.  That’s enough power for a mid size town. In reality, the power consumption will be much higher. Juno power consumption could easy blow out 100’s of megawatt hours of electricity in short order. <P>This problem is not unique to the Juno software. The default installations of all the distributed projects have the same problem. Seti@home is just as guilty. The difference is in number of users and the fact Juno is forcing you to comply where Seti is entirely voluntary.<P>The only distributed client I am aware of that can be configured so that it doesn’t interfere with power management is the one from Distributed.net.  Unlike most of the others, it is possible to configure Distributed.net’s client to use a network drive instead of C:.  This allows the Distributed.net client to operate with minimal power consumption on the desktop machines. The hard disk and monitor power down normally during periods of inactivity.<P><P>------------------<BR><I><B>Screamin Yellow Zonkers... If it's Screamin and Yellow, I ain't eatin it.</B></I>||||reg||4
